.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hardener
is a security hardening tool for Linux systems.
It scans for misconfigurations, applies recommended security settings, and generates
compliance reports against industry frameworks.
The tool supports Arch, Debian, Fedora, RHEL, and openSUSE distributions.
.PP
Eight built-in security plugins cover the following areas:
.TP
.B kernel
Sysctl parameters: ASLR, pointer restriction, ptrace scope, SYN cookies, and more.
.TP
.B ssh
OpenSSH server configuration: root login, password authentication, key exchange, and idle timeouts.
.TP
.B firewall
Firewall status and baseline rules via nftables, firewalld, or UFW.
.TP
.B pam
PAM password complexity, aging policies, and account lockout.
.TP
.B service
Detection and disabling of unnecessary network services.
.TP
.B permissions
File and directory permission auditing for critical system paths.
.TP
.B audit
Linux Audit subsystem rules for monitoring security-relevant events.
.TP
.B mac
Mandatory Access Control status (SELinux or AppArmor).
.PP
All changes are checkpointed before application, allowing safe rollback to any
previous state.
A desktop GUI (Tauri + Leptos) provides the same functionality with a graphical interface.
.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B scan
Scan the system for security issues.
Returns findings categorised by severity (info, low, medium, high, critical).
.TP
.B apply
Apply hardening recommendations.
Creates an automatic checkpoint before making changes.
.TP
.B rollback
Restore the system to a previous checkpoint.
Requires a checkpoint identifier obtained from
.BR "hardener checkpoint list" .
.TP
.B checkpoint
Manage checkpoints.
Subcommands:
.BR list ,
.BR create ,
.BR delete ,
.BR show ,
.BR repair .
.B "checkpoint repair"
reports file rows that no checkpoint owns, stranded by something other than
this tool editing the checkpoint database directly, since the schema's foreign
key is enforced on every path reached through
.BR hardener .
Reporting only is the default; pass
.B \-\-execute
to remove the orphaned rows instead.
Not host-scoped, and refuses
.BR \-\-ssh :
it mends this host's own checkpoint database, which holds the rows of every
host it has ever reached.
.TP
.B plugins
List all available security plugins with their metadata.
.TP
.B exception
Accept a single finding as a documented deviation, without hand-editing
.IR config.toml .
Subcommands:
.BR add ,
.BR remove .
Not host-scoped, and refuses
.BR \-\-ssh :
it edits this host's own configuration file, the file this host's
.B apply
reads; an exception for a remote host is written on that host.
See
.BR "Exception Options" .
.TP
.B scope
Declare a compliance control not applicable to this system, so it leaves the
compliance score's denominator instead of counting against it.
Subcommands:
.BR exclude ,
.BR include .
Distinct from
.BR exception ,
which documents a deviation the engine found: a scope exclusion covers a
control the engine cannot assess at all, and can never mute a failing control,
because a live finding, an unchecked control and an assessed control are all
decided before exclusions are consulted.
Refuses
.BR \-\-ssh ,
since it edits this host's own configuration file. That file is also the policy
the controller evaluates the fleet against, so an exclusion written here reaches
every host a
.B batch
verb touches unless
.B \-\-host
narrows it.
See
.BR "Scope Options" .
.TP
.B report
Generate compliance reports against industry security frameworks.
.TP
.B batch scan
Scan multiple remote hosts concurrently in a single run.
Hosts are taken from the inventory file
.RB ( \-\-all
or
.BR \-\-host )
or supplied ad-hoc with
.BR \-\-ssh .
Prints a per-host table and a fleet rollup, and returns a tiered exit status
suitable for CI.
See
.B Batch Scan Options
and
.BR "EXIT STATUS" .
.TP
.B batch report
Assess multiple hosts against a compliance framework or scenario preset in a
single concurrent run, printing a fleet posture table (one row per host and
framework: score and pass/fail/manual/N-A control counts) plus a per-framework
rollup.
See
.B Batch Scan Options
.RB ( \-\-all ", " \-\-host ", " \-\-ssh ", " \-\-concurrency )
and the
.B Report Options
.RB ( \-\-framework ", " \-\-profile ", " \-\-scenario ).
Tiered exit status identical to
.BR "batch scan" .
.TP
.B batch apply
Apply hardening to multiple hosts concurrently.
.BR "Dry-run by default" ,
so it validates each host and reports what would change without making any
modifications.
Pass
.B \-\-execute
to perform real changes.
Before executing on a host, the command probes for privilege (uid 0 or
passwordless
.BR sudo );
a host that fails the probe is isolated as
.I failed
and the remaining hosts are unaffected.
On
.B \-\-execute
each host receives an automatic host-keyed checkpoint (for rollback via
.BR "sudo hardener \-\-ssh <host> rollback" )
and a best-effort audit-log entry.
Two refusals happen with
.BR \-\-execute ,
both before the first connection, both exit status 2.
A
.B \-\-config
path that will not load is fatal rather than a fall-back to built-in defaults,
which would otherwise harden every host in the run against a policy nobody
selected.
And a selection whose hosts cannot file their checkpoints apart is refused: the
checkpoint host key is
.IR ssh://user@host:port ,
where a target that names no user has its account resolved through
.B "ssh \-G"
against your
.IR ~/.ssh/config ,
without opening a connection, and falls back to a literal
.I root
only when ssh itself cannot be run.
So
.B "\-\-ssh web\-01"
and
.B "\-\-ssh admin@web\-01"
share one key whenever your ssh configuration resolves
.I web\-01
to
.IR admin ,
and
.B "\-\-ssh web\-01"
and
.B "\-\-ssh root@web\-01"
share one key only when it resolves
.I web\-01
to
.IR root .
The same two refusals apply to
.B "batch rollback \-\-execute" ,
which also writes checkpoints.
See
.B Batch Apply Options
and
.BR "EXIT STATUS" .
.TP
.B batch rollback
Roll back multiple hosts concurrently to their latest per-plugin checkpoint.
.BR "Dry-run by default" ,
so it previews per host which checkpoint(s) would be restored.
Pass
.B \-\-execute
to perform the restore.
The same per-host privilege probe as
.B batch apply
gates
.BR \-\-execute ,
isolating a non-privileged host as
.I failed
while the others proceed.
Restores reuse the host-keyed checkpoints captured by
.BR "batch apply" ,
so a checkpoint is never restored onto a different host; each executed host
receives a best-effort audit-log entry.
.B \-\-plugin
selects which plugins to roll back (default: all).
See
.B Batch Apply Options
and
.BR "EXIT STATUS" .
.TP
.B daemon
Manage the scheduled scanning daemon.
Subcommands:
.BR start ,
.BR run\-once ,
.BR status .
.TP
.B systemd
Manage systemd unit files for automated scanning.
Subcommands:
.BR generate ,
.BR install ,
.BR uninstall ,
.BR status .
All four honour the global
.BR \-\-format .
Under
.B json
each prints one envelope:
.B generate
carries the two units as
.I service
and
.I timer
objects, or the paths it wrote as
.IR generated ;
.B install
carries
.I installed
and
.IR timer_enabled ,
the latter reporting what
.B "systemctl enable \-\-now"
actually returned;
.B uninstall
carries
.IR removed ,
which is empty rather than absent when there was nothing to remove, and
.IR timer_disabled ,
reporting what
.B "systemctl disable \-\-now"
returned; expect
.I false
where the timer was never enabled, since systemctl fails for a unit that does
not exist, so it is worth reading only beside a non-empty
.IR removed ;
and
.B status
carries
.IR user_mode ", " exit_code ", " stdout " and " stderr .
The exit code is carried because an inactive timer and a unit that does not
exist both make
.B systemctl
return non-zero.
.TP
.B history
View and export scan history.
Subcommands:
.BR list ,
.BR trends ,
.BR regressions ,
.BR show ,
.BR export .
.B "history trends \-\-host" " " \fIKEY\fR
shows a per-host security timeline, per-severity counts oldest scan first,
capped at
.B \-\-limit
scans (default 20).
.B "history regressions"
compares each host's two newest completed scans and reports any host whose
latest scan is worse than the previous one, limited to one host with
.BR \-\-host ;
it exits
.B 1
when a regression is found and
.B 0
when every host is stable or improving, so it can gate CI the way
.B \-\-exit\-code
gates
.BR scan .
.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Global Options
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-format " " \fIFORMAT\fR
Output format.
One of
.B text
or
.BR json ;
any other value is refused when the arguments are parsed, with exit status 2.
The CSV, HTML and PDF formatters belong to
.B \-\-report\-format
and to the interactive wizard, not to this flag.
On
.B report
this flag governs the command's own progress only, because the report body is
what
.B \-\-report\-format
selects.
Under
.B json
each
.B systemd
verb prints one envelope on standard output in place of its human text, and
.B \-\-quiet
suppresses progress but never that envelope.
Default:
.BR text .
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress non-essential output.
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-config " " \fIFILE\fR
Path to a configuration file.
For plugin policy this is an
.I addition
to the default sources, merged on top of whatever the system and user files
contributed, not a replacement for them.
For the
.B [scheduler]
section it joins the
.I front
of the search rather than replacing it, because that section is read from the
first file that configures it rather than merged: a file with no
.B [scheduler]
section is not that file configuring it, so the system or user config still
decides.
A
.I partial
.B [scheduler]
table is valid and takes the documented default for every key it omits, but the
section still does not merge, so a partial one hides a complete one in a
later-searched file and takes those defaults rather than that file's values.
.IP
Accepted anywhere, acted on by
.BR scan ,
.BR apply ,
.BR report ,
.BR "batch scan" ,
.BR "batch report" ,
.BR "batch apply" ,
.BR daemon ,
.BR history ,
.BR "exception add" ,
which loads the named file as policy before editing it,
and
.BR "systemd generate" / install ,
which embed the path in the unit they write, exactly as it was typed: give those
two an absolute path, since systemd resolves a relative one against the
service's working directory rather than yours, and regenerate the unit if the
file later moves, because the embedded path has no fallback.
.BR "exception remove"
does not load the file, but it does write to the path this flag names, so the
flag reaches it as a destination rather than as policy.
Every other command accepts it and does nothing with it.
.IP
A named path that cannot be read is fatal for the commands that write or that
read the scheduler section, rather than falling back to built-in defaults.
.B "batch scan"
and
.BR "batch report" ,
and
.B "batch apply"
without
.BR \-\-execute ,
deliberately keep that fallback and warn on standard error, because they change
no host.
.TP
.BI \-\-ssh " HOST"
Remote host to act on via SSH.
Accepts
.IR user@host
format.
Honoured by
.BR scan ,
.BR apply ,
.BR rollback ,
.BR report
and
.BR batch ,
whose own
.B \-\-ssh
is the same argument.
.B checkpoint list
and
.B checkpoint create
reach the target; the other two checkpoint verbs address a row of this host's
database by id and do not.
Refused, with exit status 2 and before any connection is opened, by
.BR daemon ,
.BR systemd ,
.BR history ,
.BR plugins ,
.BR exception ,
.BR "checkpoint show" ,
.BR "checkpoint delete" ,
and
.BR "checkpoint repair" ,
which act on this host alone.
.TP
.BI \-\-port " PORT"
SSH port number.
Applies to
.B \-\-ssh
and to any
.B batch
ad-hoc target that names no port of its own.
Only
.B batch
targets take a
.IB : port
suffix, and a valid one there outranks this flag; for every other command the
whole
.B \-\-ssh
value is the hostname, so
.I web-01:2200
is looked up as that name.
Inventory hosts carry their own port.
Default:
.BR 22 .
.TP
.BI \-\-ssh\-key " FILE"
Path to an SSH private key (identity file).
.TP
.BI \-\-ssh\-timeout " SECONDS"
SSH connection timeout in seconds.
Default:
.BR 30 .
.TP
.B \-\-ssh\-no\-verify
Skip SSH host key verification.
Use with caution.
.
.SS Scan Options
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-plugin " " \fIPLUGIN\fR
Run only the specified plugin.
May be given multiple times.
.TP
.B \-\-audit
Audit mode: perform a pure security assessment without applying changes.
.TP
.B \-\-exit\-code
Exit with status 1 if any findings exist.
Useful for CI/CD pipelines.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-severity " " \fISEVERITY\fR
Minimum severity level to include in results.
One of
.BR info ,
.BR low ,
.BR medium ,
.BR high ,
or
.BR critical .
Default:
.BR info .
.
.SS Apply Options
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-plugin " " \fIPLUGIN\fR
Apply only the specified plugin.
May be given multiple times.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-all
Apply all enabled plugins.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Preview the changes that would be made without actually applying them.
.
.SS Exception Options
.TP
.I PLUGIN_ID
Plugin the finding came from, as
.B "hardener plugins"
lists it.
.TP
.I KEY
Exception key, as
.B "hardener scan"
prints beside the finding.
.TP
.BI \-\-reason " REASON"
Why this deviation is accepted.
Required on
.BR add ;
an undocumented deviation is what an exception exists to prevent.
.TP
.BI \-\-approved\-by " NAME"
Who approved it.
.TP
.BI \-\-ticket " REF"
Approval ticket or issue reference.
.TP
.BI \-\-expires " DATE"
Date the exception stops applying (ISO 8601, e.g.
.IR 2027\-01\-31 ).
.PP
.B "exception remove"
takes only
.I PLUGIN_ID
and
.IR KEY ;
none of the flags above apply to it.
.
.SS Scope Options
These apply to
.BR "scope exclude" .
.TP
.BI \-\-reason " TEXT"
Why the control does not apply. Required: an exclusion raises the score by
leaving its denominator, and an unexplained one raises it for no stated cause.
.TP
.BI \-\-approved\-by " NAME"
Who approved it.
.TP
.BI \-\-ticket " REF"
Approval ticket or issue reference.
.TP
.BI \-\-review\-by " DATE"
Date the exclusion must be reviewed again (ISO 8601, e.g.
.IR 2027\-08\-18 ).
Absent means twelve months, which is every framework's interval here. Four
publish one that bears on a scope determination (PCI DSS Req 12.5.2, FedRAMP
CA-2, SOC 2's Type II period, and 32 CFR 170.22's annual affirmation for NIST
800-171) and the other six publish none, naming a change trigger instead, so
twelve months is this project's default rather than a framework requirement.
.B "PCI DSS service providers must set six months by hand"
under Req 12.5.2.1; the tool cannot know which an operator is.
.TP
.BI \-\-host " HOST"
A host the exclusion covers, repeatable. Matched case-insensitively against a
saved profile's name, its hostname, or its
.IR user@host:port
target. Absent means every host, which is the common case.
.PP
.I approved_date
is always written and is not a flag: without it the configuration layer treats
the exclusion as having no usable date and refuses to apply it, so an omitted
date would write a declaration that silently never took effect.
.PP
An empty reason and an unknown framework id are both refused, and both refusals
are written to the audit log, since a log recording only successes could not
show an operator attempting an exclusion they should not have.
.PP
Only CIS and ISO/IEC 27001 ship a curated control catalogue. Every other
framework's catalogue is derived from what the engine can already assess, so an
exclusion against one of those cannot take effect;
.B "scope exclude"
still writes and still audits it, and warns on standard error.
.PP
.B "scope include"
takes only
.I FRAMEWORK
and
.IR CONTROL ;
none of the flags above apply to it.
.
.SS Report Options
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-scenario " " \fISCENARIO\fR
Use-case scenario for report generation.
One of
.BR server ,
.BR workstation ,
.BR government ,
.BR healthcare ,
.BR financial ,
.BR gdpr ,
or
.BR all .
.TP
.BI \-\-framework " FRAMEWORK"
Compliance framework.
One of
.BR cis ,
.BR stig ,
.BR nist ,
.BR pcidss ,
.BR hipaa ,
.BR gdpr ,
.BR iso27001 ,
.BR soc2 ,
.BR 800\-171 ,
or
.BR fedramp .
.TP
.BI \-\-profile " PROFILE"
Compliance ID profile.
One of
.BR generic
or
.BR rhel10 .
Default: auto-detect from the target system.
.TP
.BI \-\-report\-format " FORMAT"
Report output format.
One of
.BR text " (or " txt ),
.BR json ,
.BR csv ,
.BR html ,
or
.BR pdf .
This is the only flag that reaches the CSV, HTML and PDF formatters.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIFILE\fR
Write the report to the specified file path instead of stdout.
The format's extension is added when the path has none.
A path whose extension names one of the formats this tool renders but
.I not
the one selected is refused, before the scan runs, with exit status 1 for
.B report
and 2 for the
.B batch
verbs, which count it among their usage errors: a text report is never written
into a file called
.IR report.json .
Only the six extensions this tool actually produces count
.RB ( .txt ", " .json ", " .csv ", " .htm ", " .html ", " .pdf ),
so a merely dotted name such as
.I report.2026.08.03
asks for no document and is written as given.
The same refusal covers the interactive wizard and
.BR batch 's
own
.BR \-\-output .
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interactive
Launch the interactive report wizard.
.
.SS Batch Scan Options
.TP
.B \-\-all
Scan every host in the inventory file.
Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-host .
.TP
.BI \-\-host " NAMES"
Inventory host name(s) to scan.
Accepts a comma-separated list and may be given multiple times.
.TP
.BI \-\-ssh " USER@HOST"
Ad-hoc host to scan that is not in the inventory.
May be given multiple times.
.TP
.BI \-\-concurrency " N"
Maximum number of hosts to scan in parallel.
Default:
.BR 8 .
.PP
Output honours the global
.B \-\-format
.RB ( text " or " json )
and
.B \-\-quiet
options.
.
.SS Batch Apply Options
.TP
.B \-\-all
Apply to every host in the inventory file.
Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-host .
.TP
.BI \-\-host " NAMES"
Inventory host name(s) to apply to.
Accepts a comma-separated list and may be given multiple times.
.TP
.BI \-\-ssh " USER@HOST"
Ad-hoc host not in the inventory.
Accepts an optional port suffix
.RI ( user@host:port ).
May be given multiple times.
.TP
.BI \-\-plugin " PLUGIN"
Apply only the specified plugin.
May be given multiple times.
Omit to apply all plugins.
.TP
.B \-\-execute
Perform real changes on each host.
Without this flag the command runs in dry-run mode: it validates each host and
reports what would change, but makes no modifications.
.TP
.BI \-\-concurrency " N"
Maximum number of hosts to process in parallel.
Default:
.BR 8 .
.TP
.BI \-\-output " FILE"
Write the result to the specified file path instead of stdout.
.PP
Output honours the global
.B \-\-format
.RB ( text " or " json )
and
.B \-\-quiet
options.
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/linux-hardener/config.toml
System-wide configuration file.
Settings here apply to all users and require root privileges to modify.
.TP
.I ~/.config/linux-hardener/config.toml
Per-user configuration file.
Overrides system-wide settings for the current user.
.TP
.I ~/.config/linux-hardener/hosts.toml
Host inventory for
.BR "batch scan" ,
.BR "batch report" ,
.BR "batch apply" ,
and
.BR "batch rollback" .
Shared with the desktop GUI's host list; may also be hand-edited.
.TP
.I /var/lib/linux-hardener/
System state directory.
Contains the checkpoint database and audit logs created during privileged operations.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/linux-hardener/
Per-user state directory.
Contains scan history and checkpoints for unprivileged operations.
.TP
.I /usr/etc/
Vendor configuration layer on layering distributions, openSUSE today and Fedora
moving the same way, where
.I /etc
is reserved for administrator overrides.
Where a critical path is absent from
.I /etc
the scan assesses the
.I /usr/etc
copy rather than reporting nothing, so a violating mode on a file that is
actually in force is named.
.B apply
never writes a package-owned file, because the next package update would revert
it, so such a finding is remediated by hand and the finding prints the
.BR install (1)
command that creates the
.I /etc
copy at the required mode.
An
.B apply
that appears idle on such a path is the designed outcome, not a failure.
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
All operations completed without error and, when
.B \-\-exit\-code
is not set, the exit status does not reflect whether findings exist.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred, or findings exist when
.B \-\-exit\-code
is specified.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error. In particular
.B \-\-ssh
given to a command that acts on this host alone, which is refused before any
connection is opened.
.PP
Two argument errors exit
.B 1
rather than 2, because they are detected by the command rather than by the
argument parser: for
.BR report ,
an
.B \-\-output
path whose extension contradicts
.BR \-\-report\-format ;
and a
.B \-\-config
path that will not load for
.B daemon
or
.BR history .
The
.B batch
verbs judge their own
.B \-\-output
before contacting anything and exit
.B 2
for it, with their other usage refusals.
.PP
For
.BR "batch scan" ,
.BR "batch report" ,
.BR "batch apply" ,
and
.BR "batch rollback" ,
the exit status is tiered:
.TP
.B 0
All selected hosts completed successfully with no failures or findings.
.TP
.B 1
All hosts were reachable, but at least one host has findings
.RB ( "batch scan" / "batch report" ),
an apply/validation failure
.RB ( "batch apply" ),
or a checkpoint restore failure
.RB ( "batch rollback" ).
.TP
.B 2
At least one host errored, was unreachable, or failed a privilege probe
.RB ( "batch apply" " or " "batch rollback" " with " \-\-execute ),
or a usage error occurred: no hosts selected, an unknown
.B \-\-host
name, a
.B \-\-config
path that will not load under
.BR \-\-execute ,
or a selection whose hosts would file their checkpoints under one host key.
The last two are refused before any connection is opened, so no host is
touched.
An
.B \-\-output
path naming a document other than the one
.B \-\-format
selected is refused here too, before any host is contacted.
A fleet report is text or JSON only, so a path naming CSV, HTML or PDF
contradicts it whichever way
.B \-\-format
is set.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
Scan all plugins and display results as text:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hardener scan
.fi
.RE
.PP
Scan only the SSH and kernel plugins with minimum severity of medium:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hardener scan \-p ssh \-p kernel \-s medium
.fi
.RE
.PP
Run an audit scan and exit with code 1 if findings exist (for CI/CD):
.PP
.RS
.nf
hardener scan \-\-audit \-\-exit\-code \-f json
.fi
.RE
.PP
Preview firewall hardening changes without applying them:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hardener apply \-p firewall \-\-dry\-run
.fi
.RE
.PP
Apply all hardening plugins:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo hardener apply \-\-all
.fi
.RE
.PP
List available checkpoints and roll back to a specific one:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hardener checkpoint list
sudo hardener rollback abc123
.fi
.RE
.PP
Generate a CIS Benchmark compliance report and save it as PDF:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hardener report \-\-framework cis \-\-report\-format pdf \-o report.pdf
.fi
.RE
.PP
Scan a remote host over SSH:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hardener scan \-\-ssh admin@192.168.1.100 \-\-ssh\-key ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
.fi
.RE
.PP
Scan a remote host on a non-standard SSH port:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hardener scan \-\-ssh admin@server.example.com \-\-port 2222
.fi
.RE
.PP
Scan every inventory host concurrently and emit JSON for CI:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hardener \-\-format json batch scan \-\-all
.fi
.RE
.PP
Scan two named inventory hosts with higher parallelism:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hardener batch scan \-\-host web\-01,db\-02 \-\-concurrency 16
.fi
.RE
.PP
Preview hardening changes across the entire fleet (dry-run, no modifications):
.PP
.RS
.nf
hardener batch apply \-\-all
.fi
.RE
.PP
Preview a fleet-wide rollback of the SSH hardening, then execute it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hardener batch rollback \-\-all \-\-plugin ssh
sudo hardener batch rollback \-\-all \-\-plugin ssh \-\-execute
.fi
.RE
.PP
Apply hardening to two hosts with bounded parallelism:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo hardener batch apply \-\-host web\-01,web\-02 \-\-execute \-\-concurrency 4
.fi
.RE
.PP
Apply the SSH plugin to an ad-hoc host and emit JSON output:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo hardener \-\-format json batch apply \-\-ssh admin@db\-03 \-\-plugin ssh \-\-execute
.fi
.RE
.PP
Export scan history to JSON:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hardener history export abc123 \-f json \-o history.json
.fi
.RE
.PP
Install and enable the systemd timer for daily scans:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo hardener systemd install
sudo hardener systemd status
.fi
.RE
.PP
Use a custom configuration file:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hardener scan \-C /path/to/config.toml
.fi
.RE
.
